% ============================================================================
% syllable-count — how many syllables a word has, as a teacher demonstrates it
% by placing one card per syllable (adj-facts-stdlib, LANGUAGE). CCSS RF.K.2.b
% "Count, pronounce, blend, and segment syllables in spoken words."
% ============================================================================
% A SECOND literacy sub-skill in this loop's curriculum sweep, deliberately
% DIFFERENT in shape from `word-families.adj`'s rhyme-family DERIVATION: this
% is a genuinely new phonological-awareness skill (syllable segmentation, RF.
% K.2.b) rather than another word family (RF.K.2.a). Recall is a binding
% query:
%
%     ? syllable_count(peanut, $N)      % 2  (peanut has two syllables)
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `syllable_count(word, count)` carrying the Reading Rockets
% citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the
% engine returns the count WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model
% calls, and abstains on any word the cited page does not explicitly
% segment (it never guesses a syllable count from spelling heuristics, which
% are notoriously unreliable in English). The query also runs BACKWARD — bind
% a count and recall every word the source segments to that many syllables:
%
%     ? syllable_count($W, 2)           % peanut, pencil, sunset, laptop
%
% Provenance: Reading Rockets' "Phonological and Phonemic Awareness: In
% Practice" module demonstrates syllable segmentation as a classroom
% technique — the teacher says a word, then places one index card per
% syllable while saying each part aloud. Four words are explicitly
% segmented this way on the page, and every one below is confirmed by its
% own quoted sentence (WebFetch-verified TWICE for consistency before
% writing — the first pass over-eagerly attributed a syllable count to
% "classroom" that the second, more careful pass found was NOT actually
% syllable-segmented on this page, just used in an unrelated sentence; only
% the four genuinely confirmed words below are shipped):
%
%   peanut -> 2
%     "I say the whole word: 'Peanut'. I say each syllable and put down a
%      card: 'pea' [place a card] 'nut' [place a card so it appears
%      left-to-right for students]."
%   pencil -> 2
%     "The word is: 'pencil'. 'Pen' 'cil' [place a card for each syllable
%      so it appears left-to-right for students]."
%   sunset -> 2
%     "I say the whole word: 'sunset'. I say each syllable and put down a
%      card: 'sun' [place a card] 'set' [place a card so it appears
%      left-to-right for students]."
%   laptop -> 2
%     "I say each syllable and put down a card: 'lap' [place a card] 'top'
%      [place a card so it appears left-to-right for students]."
%
% All four happen to be two-syllable words in this cited source — the page's
% classroom demonstration does not segment any one- or three-syllable word,
% so none is asserted here (a future slice can add contrast once a
% comparably clean citation for a different count is found; this is the same
% "keep each slice small and citable, only what is actually stated" discipline
% every prior curriculum item in this loop has used, even where it yields a
% narrower first slice than would be pedagogically ideal). `trust consensus`,
% not `authoritative`: Reading Rockets is a respected literacy-education
% secondary reference, not a primary/official standards body (the same tier
% `word-families.adj` already uses). (See ../README.md;
% feedback_nothing_human_authored, feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table syllable_count {
    columns word, count

    row (peanut, 2)
    row (pencil, 2)
    row (sunset, 2)
    row (laptop, 2)

    source "I say the whole word: 'Peanut'. I say each syllable and put down a card: 'pea' [place a card] 'nut' [place a card so it appears left-to-right for students]."
    locator "https://www.readingrockets.org/reading-101/reading-101-learning-modules/course-modules/phonological-and-phonemic-awareness/practice"
    trust consensus
}
